14
00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.402
Why does the scope matter
as much as the policy?

15
00:00:52.402 --> 00:00:56.233
Because Conditional Access for
workload identities is narrow,

16
00:00:56.233 --> 00:01:01.003
as Microsoft Learn's Conditional Access
for workload identities page sets out.

17
00:01:01.003 --> 00:01:03.215
Block is the only grant control.

18
00:01:03.215 --> 00:01:08.384
It covers single-tenant service principals
registered in your tenant, and nothing

19
00:01:08.384 --> 00:01:13.403
else: not Microsoft or SaaS apps, not
multitenant apps, and not managed identities.

20
00:01:13.403 --> 00:01:17.977
A policy assigned to a group that holds a
service principal isn't enforced for it;

21
00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:20.889
you have to assign the
service principal directly.

22
00:01:20.889 --> 00:01:25.278
And Workload I D Premium is needed
to create or change these policies.

23
00:01:25.278 --> 00:01:29.891
So in this module you hold the
program's two policies, C A oh fifteen

24
00:01:29.891 --> 00:01:34.590
for location and C A oh thirty-seven
for high risk, as reviewed files.

25
00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:37.870
They're scoped by object
I D, one ring at a time,

26
00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.970
and every one is created report-only.

27
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.599
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

28
00:01:47.599 --> 00:01:50.282
which takes about sixty-five
minutes with the lab.

29
00:01:50.282 --> 00:01:54.393
You'll state the four limits of
Conditional Access for workload identities,

30
00:01:54.393 --> 00:01:56.807
and what covers the
identities outside them.

31
00:01:56.807 --> 00:01:59.535
You'll explain why each policy is rendered

32
00:01:59.535 --> 00:02:02.419
once per ring and always
created report-only.

33
00:02:02.419 --> 00:02:04.245
You'll run the pre-merge check,

34
00:02:04.245 --> 00:02:07.970
the tenant check and the plan,
and read what each one reports.

35
00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:12.532
You'll read a sign-in summary and say
whether a ring is ready for enforcement.

36
00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:15.783
And you'll see a ring held
without the allow-enforce switch,

37
00:02:15.783 --> 00:02:19.033
enforced with it, and held again
when the summary is edited.

38
00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:23.435
Before you start, finish Module 5, the
other half of Gate 4, and Module 1,

39
00:02:23.435 --> 00:02:27.523
because the scope names service
principals by the enterprise

40
00:02:27.523 --> 00:02:31.340
application's object I D, as
the register records them.

41
00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:39.627
Unit 1 is the guide's Step 4,
sub-steps four d and four e.

42
00:02:39.627 --> 00:02:42.365
Four d is custom security attributes.

43
00:02:42.365 --> 00:02:47.261
You define an attribute set for applications,
such as environment and data class,

44
00:02:47.261 --> 00:02:51.865
and, as Microsoft Learn's page on
filtering for applications in Conditional

45
00:02:51.865 --> 00:02:56.177
Access says, a policy can then select
target applications by attribute.

46
00:02:56.177 --> 00:02:58.215
Only string attributes work.

47
00:02:58.215 --> 00:03:02.133
And because service principals
can't be members of dynamic groups,

48
00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:04.840
attributes are how this
track targets by fact.

49
00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:09.767
In G C C, four d waits for a tenant check
that confirms custom security attributes,

50
00:03:09.767 --> 00:03:12.454
and until then each
policy names its target

51
00:03:12.454 --> 00:03:15.440
applications and service
principals one by one.

52
00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.765
Four e is the workload
identity policies themselves.

53
00:03:18.765 --> 00:03:21.741
For each Tier 1 single-tenant
service principal,

54
00:03:21.741 --> 00:03:26.725
assign it directly to a location block that
allows only the networks it runs from,

55
00:03:26.725 --> 00:03:29.552
and to a high-risk block,
both report-only first.

56
00:03:29.552 --> 00:03:32.816
The results appear in the
service principal sign-in logs.

57
00:03:32.816 --> 00:03:35.136
Managed identities are
governed through their

58
00:03:35.136 --> 00:03:37.340
role assignments and
access reviews instead.

59
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:41.526
And Microsoft Learn's Conditional
Access for workload identities page

60
00:03:41.526 --> 00:03:45.802
says creating or changing these
policies needs Workload I D Premium.

61
00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:52.137
Unit 2 is the context and the files.

62
00:03:52.137 --> 00:03:55.125
The program's design
guidance sets two policies.

63
00:03:55.125 --> 00:03:58.796
C A oh fifteen blocks the
ring's service principals from

64
00:03:58.796 --> 00:04:02.787
anywhere but one named location,
the workload egress ranges.

65
00:04:02.787 --> 00:04:06.075
C A oh thirty-seven blocks
them when their risk is high.

66
00:04:06.075 --> 00:04:07.575
Two decisions frame them.

67
00:04:07.575 --> 00:04:10.754
D N P four is licensing:
Workload I D Premium

68
00:04:10.754 --> 00:04:14.387
for the Tier 1 single-tenant
service principals.

69
00:04:14.387 --> 00:04:18.379
D N P five is enforcement, and
it's the CISO's call: enforce,

70
00:04:18.379 --> 00:04:23.500
extend report-only, or enforce with
exclusions that are dated and owned.

71
00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:28.372
The scope file is the record: each
in-scope principal by object I D and ring,

72
00:04:28.372 --> 00:04:33.488
and each identity that's out of scope,
with the control that covers it instead.

73
00:04:33.488 --> 00:04:36.213
And each policy is rendered once per ring,

74
00:04:36.213 --> 00:04:41.425
so enforcing one ring changes one policy
and leaves the next one in report-only.

75
00:04:41.425 --> 00:04:43.490
That's the program's design.

76
00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:51.169
The pre-merge check holds nine
rules, N P W C A oh one to oh nine,

77
00:04:51.169 --> 00:04:56.818
and most come straight from the limits on
Microsoft Learn's Conditional Access for

78
00:04:56.818 --> 00:04:59.274
workload identities page; the range,

79
00:04:59.274 --> 00:05:03.203
ring and decision rules are
the program's design guidance.

80
00:05:03.203 --> 00:05:07.531
Oh one: a policy that targets
users, groups, roles, a filter,

81
00:05:07.531 --> 00:05:13.177
or every service principal in the tenant,
instead of the ring's object I D's.

82
00:05:13.177 --> 00:05:15.866
Oh two: a managed identity in scope.

83
00:05:15.866 --> 00:05:20.065
Oh three: a principal that isn't a
single-tenant app registered here.

84
00:05:20.065 --> 00:05:24.503
Oh four: an object I D that isn't
a service principal in the tenant.

85
00:05:24.503 --> 00:05:27.464
Oh five is the licensing
record and the rings.

86
00:05:27.464 --> 00:05:32.202
Oh six: an out-of-scope identity recorded
without the control that covers it.

87
00:05:32.202 --> 00:05:36.130
Oh seven: a policy that isn't
report-only and block-only,

88
00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:39.253
for all resources and
all client app types.

89
00:05:39.253 --> 00:05:42.927
Oh eight keeps the egress
ranges narrow and untrusted.

90
00:05:42.927 --> 00:05:44.865
And oh nine guards enforcement.

91
00:05:44.865 --> 00:05:48.520
A finding exits with code
one and fails the pipeline.

92
00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:54.295
Unit 3 begins with the plan.

93
00:05:54.295 --> 00:05:59.233
It compares the files with the tenant
and gives each policy one of six actions.

94
00:05:59.233 --> 00:06:02.071
Create, when the policy
isn't in the tenant,

95
00:06:02.071 --> 00:06:06.996
and a new policy is always created
report-only, whatever its ring's state.

96
00:06:06.996 --> 00:06:11.770
Update, when it differs; the patch
carries only the properties that changed.

97
00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:13.820
No change, when it matches.

98
00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.727
Blocked, for a location policy whose named

99
00:06:16.727 --> 00:06:20.132
location doesn't exist yet;
the next run creates it.

100
00:06:20.132 --> 00:06:24.896
Held, when the ring is marked for
enforcement but this run may not enforce it.

101
00:06:24.896 --> 00:06:28.868
And not in repository, for a
workload policy that no file names,

102
00:06:28.868 --> 00:06:31.283
such as one someone made in the portal.

103
00:06:31.283 --> 00:06:33.220
It's reported and left alone.

104
00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:35.083
The code has no delete at all.

105
00:06:35.083 --> 00:06:39.188
Rollback drops the ring from the
enforcement record and applies,

106
00:06:39.188 --> 00:06:41.910
so its policies go back to report-only.

107
00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:49.266
Turning a policy on is the moment
a production job can stop,

108
00:06:49.266 --> 00:06:53.022
so the code asks for five things
at once, or the ring stays held.

109
00:06:53.022 --> 00:06:55.834
A D N P five record marks the ring.

110
00:06:55.834 --> 00:06:57.748
The change says allow enforce.

111
00:06:57.748 --> 00:07:01.897
The run names a sign-in summary
whose hash the ring's record cites.

112
00:07:01.897 --> 00:07:03.923
That summary shows the ring ready.

113
00:07:03.923 --> 00:07:06.759
And every earlier ring
is already enforced.

114
00:07:06.759 --> 00:07:09.760
The five conditions are the
program's design guidance.

115
00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:12.122
